Bugfix: Reject release artifacts that ship development files

The release workflow now fails when a tarball it is about to publish contains the
working tree the app was built in - `.git` at any depth, or `.git`, `.github`,
`tests`, `build` or `vendor-bin` at the app's own top level. The directory names
are anchored to the top level so a vendored dependency shipping its own `tests/`
is unaffected.

Release 11.0.0 shipped 13 bundled apps this way: roughly 102 MB of development
material, 16 git repositories, and `files_antivirus`'s EICAR acceptance data,
which made anti-virus scans of the release tarball fail. Because the signature
assertion runs over the same tarball, those files were code-signed too, so an
administrator could not remove them without breaking `occ integrity:check-app` -
which is why this has to be caught before the release is published rather than
cleaned up afterwards.

This complements the ocsign checkout guard: ocsign refuses to sign a staging tree
holding a `.git` entry, while this inspects the finished tarball and also covers
`tests`, `build`, `.github` and `vendor-bin`, which ocsign accepts.

